3.text:
For one instrument the Nagar first order bias can be written, after the
usual normalisations $E(z^{2})=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta=\frac{\,n^{-1}\sum z_{i}u_{i}\,}
{\pi_{n}+n^{-1}\sum z_{i}v_{i}}
      =\frac{a_{n}}{\gamma +c_{n}} ,
\qquad 
\pi_{n}=\gamma n^{-1/2},
\end{equation*}
with $a_{n},c_{n}\xrightarrow{d}N(0,\cdot )$ and 
${\rm Cov}(a_{n},c_{n})=\sigma_{uv}$.  Conditioning on $c_{n}$,

\begin{equation*}
E\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\mid c_{n}\big]
    =\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;
      \frac{c_{n}}{\gamma +c_{n}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the weak–instrument local alternative
$\pi_{n}=\gamma n^{-1/2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{E[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}
     {E[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}
   =\frac{\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}E\!\left[d/(\mu+d)\right]}
          {\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}}
   =E\!\left[\frac{d}{\mu+d}\right]
   =-\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}+O\!\left(\mu^{-4}\right),
\end{equation*}
where $d\sim N(0,1)$ and $\mu^{2}=\gamma^{2}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ is the
concentration parameter.  Up to the usual $O(\mu^{-2})$ Nagar
approximation

\begin{equation*}
\rho\equiv
\frac{\big|{\rm Bias}_{IV}\big|}
     {\big|{\rm Bias}_{OLS}\big|}
\;\approx\;\frac{1}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
E[F_{1}]\approx\mu^{2}+1\approx\mu^{2} .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the $+1$ degrees of freedom term, $\rho\approx F^{-1}$ and
the usual $10\%$ bias rule is $F\approx 10$.

Let $\lambda=\mu^{2}$.  On the weak-instrument limit
$F\stackrel{d}{\to}\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)$.  The $5\%$ lower confidence
limit for $\lambda$ is the $\lambda_{0.05}$ which satisfies
$P\!\left\{F\ge F_{obs}\mid\lambda_{0.05}\right\}=0.05$.
On the Nagar first order scale $\rho(\lambda)=1/\lambda$, so that
$\rho(\lambda_{0.05})=0.10$ is $\lambda_{0.05}=10$.  The $95\%$ quantile
of a non-central $\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda=10)$ is

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}(\lambda=10)\approx\bigl(\sqrt{\lambda}+z_{0.95}\bigr)^{2}
      \approx\bigl(3.16+1.64\bigr)^{2}\approx 2.3\!-\!2.5\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
and in the usual Stock–Yogo normalisation $E[F]=\lambda+1$ this is the
familiar $10\%$ maximal-relative-bias critical value

\begin{equation*}
F_{10\%}^{\,{\rm max\;rel\;bias}}\approx 16.4,
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $F\gtrsim 16\!-\!17$ first-stage $F$ is required for the
Nagar $10\%$ relative bias to be below $0.10$ with the conventional
$5\%$ size, $95\%$ confidence, criterion.  In the textbook $F\approx
1/\rho$ normalisation this is the $F\approx10$ rule, the $16$–$17$
value being the conventional $95\%$ Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal relative
bias critical value, commonly quoted as $F\approx 16.4$, i.e. $ \approx
16$, about $17$ on the conservative round-up.

