2.text:
In the homoscedastic just identified normal limit the Nagar first order
relative 2SLS bias is

\begin{equation*}
R_{N}(\mu^{2})=\frac{{\rm Bias}_{2SLS}}{{\rm Bias}_{OLS}}
\approx\frac{k}{\mu^{2}},\qquad k=1,
\end{equation*}

with the usual weak instrument normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}{\rm Var}(Z)/\sigma_{v}^{2},\qquad 
E[F_{1}]=\mu^{2}+1,\qquad\mu^{2}\approx F_{1}-1 ,
\end{equation*}

so that to first order

\begin{equation*}
R_{N}\simeq\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\simeq\frac{1}{F_{1}-1}\simeq\frac1{F_{1}} .
\end{equation*}

Put $t_{1}=\sqrt{F_{1}}$.  In the local weak instrument limit

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}\;\dot\sim\;{\cal N}\!\big(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1\big)+o_{p}(1),
\end{equation*}

so that on the normal scale a one sided $1-\alpha$ lower confidence
bound for $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{\mu^{2}}_{1-\alpha}\approx t_{1}-z_{1-\alpha},\qquad 
z_{0.95}=1.645 .
\end{equation*}

Hence a $1-\alpha$ lower bound for the Nagar relative bias is

\begin{equation*}
R_{1-\alpha}\approx
\frac{1}{\big[t_{1}-z_{1-\alpha}\big]^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

The point Nagar $10\%$ requirement is $ \mu^{-2}=0.10$ i.e.
$\sqrt{\mu^{2}}=\sqrt{10}=3.162$.  Imposing $R_{0.95}=0.10$,

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}^{*}-z_{0.95}= \sqrt{10},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}^{*}= \sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\approx 3.162+1.645\approx 4.81,
\end{equation*}

and on the $t_{1}\sim{\cal N}(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1)$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}^{*}=t_{1}^{*2}=\big(\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\big)^{2}
      \approx4.81^{2}\approx 23.1 ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sqrt{F_{1}}\gtrsim\sqrt{10}+1.645$.  This is the $95\%$ one sided
normal analogue of the textbook point rule $R_{N}\approx1/F_{1}<0.10\iff
F_{1}\approx10$, $F_{1}-1\approx10\iff F_{1}\approx11$.

On the conventional $ \mu^{2}\approx F_{1}-1$ degrees of freedom
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{1}-1}\gtrsim\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\iff
F_{1}\gtrsim1+\big(\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\big)^{2}\approx 24,
\end{equation*}

$F_{1}\approx22\!-\!24$, nominally $F_{1}\approx23\!-\!24$ in integer
practice, the $2\!-\!2.5\times10$ $95\%$ lower confidence upgrade of
the point Nagar rule.  With the conventional two sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$
this is $F^{*}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx26$.

Remark.  On the exact non central $\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)$ limit
$F_{1}\to\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)/1$, $\lambda=\mu^{2}$, the $5\%$ test
inversion $P\big[\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda=10)\ge F^{*}\big]=0.05$ is the
Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal relative bias critical value

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\big(\chi^{2}_{1}(10)\big)\approx 16.4\approx16\!-\!17,
\end{equation*}

which is the exact Stock–Yogo limit of the same calculation and
reduces to the point Nagar $F\approx10$, $F-1\approx10$ rule for
$z\to0$.  This is the tabulated $F_{10\%}^{\max}\simeq16.38$ value
commonly quoted in the weak instrument literature.

